README.md:69:**Fail-closed context:** `app.marks.setKeeperContext(hostId, { streamId, keeperId, optedIn, holdsRelic, blockedUserIds })` requires an explicit authoritative session ID, numeric Keeper ID, boolean consent/ownership, and a bounded configured blocked-user list. Acceptance requires that the Keeper ID equals the signed chat broadcaster, consent is true and the Keeper holds the relic. Only a trusted lifecycle/ownership integration may call this method. Tests inject synthetic trusted contexts; live stream lifecycle and ownership/opt-in synchronization are **not wired yet**. Runtime startup supplies no context, so real marks remain disabled. No ID is inferred from dates, raid events, names or viewer text. Restoring the same retained stream ID preserves its spent quota.
README.md:75:**Atomic quotas:** one accepted mark per user per 30 seconds in the configured Keeper channel, across stream transitions; five accepted marks total per broadcaster stream. The application currently supports one configured streamer. Distinct events, retries and concurrency share the same transactional event-dedupe + cooldown + spent-slot + mark mutation. Memory executes the same pure reducer synchronously; Firestore reads all required documents before writing in one transaction. Rejected submissions are acknowledged/deduplicated without spending a slot. Veto, block, opt-out and forgetting never refund spent slots. Changing to a genuinely new authoritative stream creates a fresh five-slot budget; replaying a retained session ID cannot reset it.
README.md:77:Trusted services are `approve(host, actor, markId)`, `vetoLast(host, actor)`, `pause(host, actor, paused)`, `blockUser(host, actor, userId)`, `optOut(host, actor)` and self-only `forgetMe(host, actor)`. Signed chat commands are:
README.md:83:- `!relic forgetme` with **no target argument**, using only the authenticated EventSub chatter identity.
README.md:85:All commands except self-forget require the signed broadcaster's own user ID or the signed event's `moderator` badge with ID `1`. Message text, fragments, VIP badges, or claimed role strings confer no authority. Repeated signed command delivery is deduplicated too. Service callers are trusted code and must supply only identities obtained from authenticated sources; there is no application admin login/API in this milestone.
README.md:89:Self-forget removes that chatter's retained pending/approved marks across all retained streams, their stored mark text/association, cooldown identity, and their latest ordinary-chat receipt if still present. There are no separate mark pseudonyms. Only anonymous spent counters/event expiry records remain from those marks; an anonymous 30-second channel-wide hold prevents immediate cooldown bypass without retaining a forgotten identity. Other users are also held briefly during that interval. The chatter can submit new data after that hold. Independently configured channel/moderation safety block lists and OAuth/configuration data are not erased by mark forgetting. This removes retained application mark data in the configured channel; it does **not** erase Twitch chat, logs/backups held elsewhere, or all personal data everywhere.
README.md:91:**Hard storage bounds:** each channel document retains at most 16 explicit stream sessions, each with at most five mark slots, two block lists capped at 128 numeric IDs each, at most 128 short-lived cooldown entries, and a 128 KiB serialized-document guard. IDs/text are length-bounded; this keeps the bounded ledger far below Firestore's 1 MiB document limit. At capacity, new sessions/blocks fail closed rather than silently dropping quotas or safety policy. Retention cleanup and archival expansion are pending; a 17th retained session requires a future reviewed retention policy. This per-channel ledger intentionally trades throughput for simple atomicity at prototype scale. No raw chat command text, actor identity, or pending mark text is written into event dedupe documents or public output.
README.md:93:Tests cover normalization, unsafe inputs, original rune determinism, missing/mismatched context, cooldown boundaries, concurrent five-slot limits and duplicate deliveries, session reuse/reset, moderator authorization, privacy projection, pause/blocks/opt-out, self-forget, command idempotency, bounded documents, and Firestore commit rollback/retry. Firestore tests still use the staged transactional fake; real database/emulator integration remains untested.
